(Q6) What is the difference between while and until loops in bash?


Feature
while Loop
until Loop
Condition Check
Continues while condition is true
Continues until condition becomes true
Loop Stops When
The condition evaluates to false
The condition evaluates to true
Use Case
When you want to loop as long as a condition holds
When you want to loop until a condition is met
Logic Type
Pre-check loop (checks before executing)
Same (checks before executing)
Control Flow
Starts only if the condition is initially true
Starts even if the condition is initially false
Example
while [ $x -lt 5 ]
until [ $x -ge 5 ]
Common Use
Polling, reading input, waiting for state
Waiting for completion, retrying until success

(Q7) Write a countdown timer script using a while loop

#!/bin/bash

# Prompt the user for countdown time in seconds
read -p "Enter countdown time in seconds: " time

# Check if input is a valid positive integer
if ! [[ "$time" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then
    echo "Invalid input. Please enter a positive integer."
    exit 1
fi

# Countdown loop
while [ $time -gt 0 ]; do
    echo -ne "Time remaining: $time\033[0K\r"  # \r to overwrite line
    sleep 1
    ((time--))
done

echo "Time's up! "

(Q8) How do you use break and continue statements in loops? Give examples.

Break:
    • Purpose: Immediately exits the loop.
    • Use when: You want to stop the loop when a certain condition is met
      code:
      #!/bin/bash
      for i in {1..5}; do
          if [ $i -eq 3 ]; then
            echo "Breaking at i = $i"
              break
         fi
        echo "i = $i"
      done
continue:
    • Purpose: Skips the rest of the current loop iteration and continues with the next iteration.
    • Use when: You want to skip specific cases without exiting the loop.
  code:
#!/bin/bash

for i in {1..5}; do
    if [ $i -eq 3 ]; then
        echo "Skipping i = $i"
        continue
    fi
    echo "i = $i"
done
